@page rh="HOUSE AUREM · GAVIN · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE" m="EXOD 28.2; EXOD 31.1–5; ESTH 2.12; MATT 6.28–29; VOL. I · {v1:x-calf} · {v1:esther}"
# Faith and scripture
rec:
What he believes | OPEN QUESTION: Nothing on file. INFERENCE: Fourteen years of making girls look chosen has taught him something about what the Empire means by chosen.
What the Temple teaches about a man like him | POSSIBLE: A craftsman serving the House is honored as a steward of beauty, and never asked what he's dressing girls for.
His name | *Gavin* has no biblical source. It's a Scottish and Welsh name, from the knight Gawain.
The priest's garments · Exodus 28 | POSSIBLE: Aaron's garments are made "for glory and for beauty" (28.2), and Bezalel is filled with the spirit of God "in wisdom… and in all manner of workmanship" (31.3). **Where it breaks:** Those clothes dress a priest for God. Gavin's dress a girl for a camera.
Esther's year · Esther 2.12 | POSSIBLE: Twelve months of preparation, six with oil of myrrh and six with sweet odours. **Where it breaks:** The king's women had no say in it. Eden knows exactly what the dress is for.
tab:
| He'd quote | Never read to him |
| _ | Matthew 6.28–29, the lilies of the field, "even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of these" |
